{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 15, :comments ["<b>And the king of Egypt said to the Hebrew midwives, etc.</b> Some say the midwives were Egyptian, as if it said 'to the midwives of the Hebrew women,' for otherwise what is the force of its saying 'and the midwives feared Elohim'? And our Sages of blessed memory said (Sotah 11b): Shifrah — this is Jochebed, and Puah — this is Miriam, etc. And one may reflect: perhaps the reverse — that Puah is Jochebed and Shifrah is Miriam? And what did Scripture tell us by this, that their names were changed? And it appears to me that Puah is a term of speech, and this indicates plainly concerning Miriam that she was a prophetess, as our Sages of blessed memory said (Megillah 14a): 'And Miriam the prophetess, the sister of Aaron, took' (Exodus 15:20) — that she would prophesy, 'My mother is destined to give birth to a son who will be the deliverer of Israel.' And in the portion of Va'era (Exodus 7:1) Rashi explained that prophecy is a term of speech, derived from the language of 'creating the fruit of the lips' (Isaiah 57:19). Jochebed — that is Shifrah, because she returned to her beauty and youth when she was one hundred and thirty years old, for then her beauty was like the beauty of her youthful days, as happened to Sarah. And this miracle too was a sign that from her would be born a son who would be Israel's deliverer, for not in vain was this miracle performed for her. And therefore Scripture told us to say: see how the counsel of Pharaoh and all his magicians was consumed, for he called the midwives who were designated by the names Shifrah and Puah, which point to the redeemer who would come forth from them — and they themselves are the ones who would give birth to the redeemer — so how could they put their own children to death? And Pharaoh did not notice the change of their names, to say: is this not a significant thing? And further, Scripture told us their names, for they were an indication that they were not in doubt about the redemption, for they had cause to doubt it, as it is written in Ezekiel (Ezekiel 20:8): 'And I said to pour out My fury upon them, to spend My anger upon them in the midst of the land of Egypt.' And these names point to the birth of the redeemer, and for this reason they did not do as Pharaoh spoke to them, for had they been in doubt, perhaps they would have consented to Pharaoh's decree, since it would be better for them that the little ones die than that they be born to vanity and confusion and live all their days in travail and servitude." "And as for what it wrote: 'And if it is a daughter, she shall live' — which seems superfluous, it appears that it is so that one not understand 'If it is a son' in the sense of 'even though,' as in 'And if the Jubilee should come' (Leviticus 25:28), which means 'even if the Jubilee comes' — so too one could understand 'If it is a son' as 'even if it is a son' who is needed for labor — nevertheless you shall put him to death, and all the more so a daughter. Therefore Scripture says: 'And if it is a daughter, she shall live.'"], :book :exodus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 1, :notes {0 "The phrase שֶׁחָזְרָה לְשׁוּפְרָהּ is rendered as 'because she returned to her beauty,' reading שֶׁ as causal on the name שִׁפְרָה."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:17:46.839531379Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}